Study Resource — Multiverse 3D XR Learning Platform

Grounded in: Google ARCore Fundamentals — Motion Tracking



Learning Objectives

Explain what motion tracking means in the context of a handheld AR device.

Describe how ARCore determines a device's position and orientation using SLAM and visual-inertial odometry.

Connect the abstract idea of X/Y/Z coordinates to the concrete mechanism a phone uses to know where it is.

How a Device Knows Where It Is

ARCore's documentation frames motion tracking as one of two things the platform is fundamentally doing at all times: tracking the position of the device as it moves, and separately building an understanding of the real world around it. Motion tracking is the first half of that pair — it is what lets the phone report its own pose (position and orientation) relative to the world, continuously, as it moves.

Technically, ARCore accomplishes this with a process called simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM). The device's camera identifies visually distinct points in the environment, called feature points, and tracks how those points shift from frame to frame. That visual information is fused with readings from the phone's inertial sensors (accelerometer and gyroscope) — an approach commonly described as visual-inertial odometry (VIO). Combining camera-based feature tracking with inertial sensor data lets the system estimate motion smoothly even during brief moments when the camera view is poor (fast motion, motion blur, or a plain-looking surface with few features).

The result of this fusion is often described as 6 degrees of freedom, or a 'pose': three values for position (X, Y, Z) and three for orientation (pitch, yaw, roll). Because tracking is continuous and relative to where the session started, an object placed in the world stays where it was left — even if the user walks away and comes back — as long as tracking has not been lost.

Why Coordinates Are Not Just Abstract Math

A common way this concept is introduced to beginners is by pointing out that 'coordinates' stop being an abstract math topic once you realize they are the literal numbers a phone is computing, many times per second, from camera and sensor data, just to know where it is pointed. The X/Y/Z position of a virtual object is not arbitrary — it's expressed in the same coordinate space the device's own tracked pose lives in, which is why moving the device changes what appears to move on screen.

Code Concepts & Examples

These snippets illustrate the underlying idea in plain JavaScript / React Three Fiber, the same environment used by the platform's in-browser editor. They are simplified teaching examples, not the completion-check solution itself.

Reading and displaying live X/Y/Z position

useFrame runs every rendered frame, mirroring how a real device's pose is re-estimated continuously rather than once.

function TrackedIcon() {

  const meshRef = useRef();

  const [pos, setPos] = useState([0, 0, 0]);



  useFrame(() => {

    if (meshRef.current) {

      const { x, y, z } = meshRef.current.position;

      setPos([x.toFixed(2), y.toFixed(2), z.toFixed(2)]);

    }

  });



  return (

    <>

      <mesh ref={meshRef} onPointerMove={handleDrag}>

        <coneGeometry args={[0.2, 0.4, 8]} />

        <meshStandardMaterial color="cyan" />

      </mesh>

      <Html position={[0, 1, 0]}>

        X: {pos[0]}  Y: {pos[1]}  Z: {pos[2]}

      </Html>

    </>

  );

}



Task: move the box to a target position with tolerance

This is the pattern the completion check uses — compare a live position against a target, within a small tolerance, rather than requiring an exact match.

const target = new THREE.Vector3(3, 2, -1);

const tolerance = 0.15;



function checkComplete(boxPosition) {

  const distance = boxPosition.distanceTo(target);

  return distance <= tolerance;

}



// Example: animate the box toward the target over time

function moveTowardTarget(box, delta) {

  box.position.lerp(target, delta * 2);

  if (checkComplete(box.position)) {

    markTaskComplete();

  }

}



Key Terms

Motion tracking

The continuous estimation of a device's position and orientation relative to the world as it moves.

SLAM

Simultaneous Localization and Mapping — the general technique of building a map of an unknown environment while simultaneously tracking a device's location within it.

Feature point

A visually distinct point in the camera image (e.g. a high-contrast corner or edge) that the system can recognize and track across frames.

Visual-inertial odometry (VIO)

A tracking method that combines camera-based feature tracking with data from motion sensors (accelerometer, gyroscope) to estimate position and orientation.

Pose

A device or object's full 6-degrees-of-freedom state: X/Y/Z position plus pitch/yaw/roll orientation.



Common Pitfalls

Comparing floating-point positions with strict equality (===) instead of a distance/tolerance check — sensor-driven and animated positions almost never land on an exact value.

Forgetting that position is relative to the current tracked origin, not a fixed universal frame — this is why real AR systems warn against reusing coordinates outside the frame they were captured in (see Module 3).

How This Grounds the Module's Visualization & Task

The module's visualization — a draggable camera-icon object with live X/Y/Z readouts on each axis and a trailing path line — is a direct stand-in for what ARCore's SLAM/VIO pipeline is doing internally: continuously producing a position in space and exposing it as numbers. Dragging the icon and watching the readouts update mirrors, at a simplified interaction level, how a real device's tracked pose changes as it moves through a room.

The coding task (moving a box to a specific target position such as (3, 2, −1)) is where the 'coordinates are not abstract' idea in the source material becomes concrete: the learner is directly manipulating the same X/Y/Z values that a real AR system reports for its own tracked pose, reinforcing that a target position is just a point in the same coordinate space the device itself is tracked in.

