Grounded in: Google ARCore Fundamentals — Environmental Understanding / Plane Classification



Learning Objectives

Explain the difference between detecting that a flat surface exists and classifying what kind of surface it is.

List the standard plane classification labels used in AR systems (floor, wall, table, ceiling, etc.).

Describe how classification enables conditional, semantically-aware placement logic.

From Geometry to Semantics

Basic plane detection (covered conceptually in Module 3) tells an AR system that a flat surface exists and roughly where its boundary is, by clustering feature points that lie on a common horizontal or vertical surface. Plane classification is a further step: modern AR platforms attach a semantic label to a detected plane, describing what kind of real-world surface it actually is. Android's ARCore-for-Jetpack-XR API, for example, exposes each detected plane with a `label` property that can be WALL, FLOOR, CEILING, or TABLE, alongside its pose (position and orientation) and extents (size). Apple's ARKit ships an equivalent set of classifications for its own plane anchors.

At the lower level, planes are also commonly distinguished by orientation: a horizontal plane facing upward (a floor or tabletop), a horizontal plane facing downward (a ceiling), and a vertical plane (a wall). Classification builds semantic labels like 'wall' or 'table' on top of this geometric foundation.

Why It Matters for Placement

Once a system knows not just that a surface exists but what kind of surface it is, an application can make placement decisions that respect real-world logic — official design guidance for AR content placement stresses that giving users visual feedback (highlights, color cues) while they search for and confirm a valid surface materially improves the experience. A picture frame conceptually belongs on a wall, not a floor; a virtual lamp belongs on a table, not a ceiling. Classification is what allows an application to encode that kind of common-sense rule as conditional logic instead of leaving placement decisions entirely up to the user.

It's also worth noting a real hardware constraint documented across multiple sources: vertical plane detection (walls) is generally harder for phone-based AR systems than horizontal plane detection (floors, tables), because most phones lack the specialized depth sensors that would make vertical surfaces easy to resolve — so classification quality can vary meaningfully by surface type and device.

Code Concepts & Examples

These snippets illustrate the underlying idea in plain JavaScript / React Three Fiber, the same environment used by the platform's in-browser editor. They are simplified teaching examples, not the completion-check solution itself.

Conditional placement based on plane classification

This is the core logic pattern behind the task: check a semantic label before allowing placement, and give immediate color feedback.

const PLANE_TYPES = {

  FLOOR: 'floor',

  TABLE: 'table',

  WALL: 'wall',

};



function canPlaceOn(objectRequiredType, surfaceClassification) {

  return objectRequiredType === surfaceClassification;

}



function handleDragOverSurface(objectType, surface) {

  const isValid = canPlaceOn(objectType, surface.classification);

  setIndicatorColor(isValid ? 'green' : 'red');

  return isValid;

}



// Example: a picture frame requires a wall

const pictureFrameRequirement = PLANE_TYPES.WALL;



function onDrop(surface) {

  if (canPlaceOn(pictureFrameRequirement, surface.classification)) {

    attachObjectToSurface(surface);

  } else {

    rejectPlacement(surface); // visible feedback, object bounces back

  }

}



Key Terms

Plane detection

Identifying that a cluster of feature points lies on a common flat surface and determining its boundary.

Plane classification

Attaching a semantic label (floor, wall, table, ceiling, etc.) to a detected plane.

HORIZONTAL_UPWARD_FACING

A classification for a horizontal plane whose surface faces up, such as a floor or tabletop.

HORIZONTAL_DOWNWARD_FACING

A classification for a horizontal plane whose surface faces down, such as a ceiling.

VERTICAL

A classification for a vertical plane, such as a wall.



Common Pitfalls

Only checking whether a surface is horizontal or vertical, and stopping there — real placement logic usually needs the finer-grained label (floor vs. table, both horizontal-upward) because they're not interchangeable placement targets.

Forgetting to give feedback on invalid targets — the color-coded indicator in this module exists specifically because silent rejection feels like a bug to the user, not a rule.

How This Grounds the Module's Visualization & Task

The module's visualization — three simultaneously labeled plane types (floor, table, wall) with a live status indicator that turns green or red depending on whether the surface under a dragged object is a valid placement target — is a direct simulation of what a classification-aware placement system does under the hood: continuously check a candidate surface's label against a required type and give the user immediate feedback, echoing the design guidance around visual cues for successful placement.

The coding task (only allow placement if the classification matches a required type, e.g. a picture frame requiring 'wall') is a simplified version of the exact conditional logic real AR placement systems implement once a plane's semantic label is available — turning environmental understanding from passive detection into an active constraint on where content is allowed to go.

