Grounded in: Google ARCore Depth API & Persistent Cloud Anchors; Apple ARKit World Mapping



Learning Objectives

Explain how depth-based occlusion lets real objects correctly hide virtual ones.

Distinguish a session-local anchor (Module 3) from a persistent or cloud anchor.

Describe how Cloud Anchors enable shared and returning AR experiences across sessions and devices.

Depth and Occlusion

ARCore's Depth API produces depth maps — images encoding, for each pixel, the distance from the device to the corresponding real-world surface, computed from the main RGB camera on supported devices. A key application of this depth data is real-time occlusion: by comparing the depth of a real surface at a given screen pixel against the depth of a virtual object at that same pixel, a shader can decide whether the real surface is actually closer to the camera, and if so, hide the virtual object behind it rather than rendering it on top regardless of distance. Without this comparison, virtual content always renders in front of everything else, which breaks the illusion that it exists in the same physical space as real objects.

Depth data has uses beyond occlusion as well — it supports more physically plausible interactions, such as collision checks between virtual elements and real surfaces, and can improve gesture or hand tracking by giving the system 3D context rather than a flat 2D image to interpret.

From Session Anchors to Persistence

A regular anchor (as covered in Module 3) keeps an object from drifting during a single AR session, but that anchor's information does not automatically outlive the session — restart the app, and that spatial reference is gone. Persistent Cloud Anchors solve this by hosting an anchor's data on a cloud endpoint: hosting an anchor uploads its pose data and returns a unique Cloud Anchor ID, and any device — the same one later, or a completely different one — can then resolve that ID against its own live view of the environment to recreate the exact same anchor and, with it, the object placed there. A regular anchor's underlying coordinates are only meaningful within the session that produced them; a Cloud Anchor is what turns that session-local reference into something durable enough to be looked up again later, by the same device or a different one.

This is what makes two distinct capabilities possible: an object left in a room can still be found there the next day (persistence across time), and multiple people can view or manipulate the same virtual object anchored to the same real location at the same time (sharing across devices). Persistent Cloud Anchors specifically extended the original 24-hour resolution window of standard Cloud Anchors to a configurable lifespan of up to 365 days.

Code Concepts & Examples

These snippets illustrate the underlying idea in plain JavaScript / React Three Fiber, the same environment used by the platform's in-browser editor. They are simplified teaching examples, not the completion-check solution itself.

Depth-based occlusion check

This is the simplified version of the per-pixel depth comparison a real occlusion shader performs, applied at the object level instead of per-pixel.

function isOccluded(boxZ, wallZ, cameraZ) {

  // Smaller distance-to-camera means closer to the camera.

  const boxDistance = Math.abs(boxZ - cameraZ);

  const wallDistance = Math.abs(wallZ - cameraZ);

  return wallDistance < boxDistance; // wall is closer -> box is hidden

}



function updateOcclusion(boxRef, wallZ, cameraZ, occlusionEnabled) {

  if (!occlusionEnabled) {

    boxRef.current.visible = true; // always renders on top, ignoring depth

    return;

  }

  boxRef.current.visible = !isOccluded(boxRef.current.position.z, wallZ, cameraZ);

}



Hosting and resolving a persistent (cloud) anchor — conceptual API shape

Simplified to show the two-step hosting/resolving pattern; actual cloud anchor calls are async and platform-specific.

async function hostAnchor(localAnchor) {

  const cloudAnchorId = await cloudAnchorService.host(localAnchor, {

    lifespanDays: 365,

  });

  saveIdForLater(cloudAnchorId);

  return cloudAnchorId;

}



async function resolveAnchor(cloudAnchorId) {

  const anchor = await cloudAnchorService.resolve(cloudAnchorId);

  return anchor; // same real-world location, possibly a different device/session

}



Key Terms

Depth map

An image where each pixel encodes the estimated distance from the camera to the corresponding real-world surface.

Occlusion

Correctly hiding part of a virtual object behind a real object that is physically closer to the camera.

Cloud Anchor

An anchor whose pose data is hosted on a cloud endpoint so it can be resolved by other devices or sessions later.

Hosting (an anchor)

Uploading an anchor's pose data to the cloud to obtain a unique Cloud Anchor ID.

Resolving (an anchor)

Using a Cloud Anchor ID, together with a live view of the environment, to recreate a previously hosted anchor on another device or at a later time.



Common Pitfalls

Comparing raw screen-space depth values without accounting for the camera's near/far range — depth comparisons need to be in a consistent space (world distance, not raw buffer values) or occlusion looks inverted.

Confusing a persistent/cloud anchor with a regular session anchor — a regular anchor from Module 3 has no ID that can be resolved later or by another device; only a hosted anchor does.

How This Grounds the Module's Visualization & Task

The module's visualization — a real-world 'wall' object between the camera and a virtual box, where dragging the box behind the wall makes it visually disappear where blocked, contrasted with a toggle that disables occlusion and shows the box incorrectly rendering on top of the wall regardless of distance — directly demonstrates the depth-comparison mechanism the Depth API uses for real-time occlusion, made visible instead of left as an abstract shader operation.

The task (testing whether the box's Z-position places it behind or in front of the wall, and enabling or disabling occlusion rendering accordingly) is a simplified stand-in for exactly the per-pixel depth comparison a real occlusion shader performs, while the module's persistence discussion sets up why the same anchor concept from Module 3 needs a cloud-hosted variant to survive beyond a single session or a single device.

