Grounded in: Unity XR Interaction Toolkit documentation (grab, activate, ray interactors — referenced conceptually); Extended Reality for Everybody — University of Michigan



Learning Objectives

Explain how real XR input differs structurally from a mouse click.

Describe the Interactor / Interactable relationship used by Unity's XR Interaction Toolkit.

Identify the components required to make an object grabbable.

Real XR Input Is a Reach and a Grab, Not a Click

Unity's XR Interaction Toolkit is built around a small set of core roles rather than a single click event: an Interactor is a component controlling how a GameObject interacts with others in the scene, and an Interactable is a component defining how the user can interact with a given object (grabbing, moving, dropping, throwing, and so on). An Interaction Manager ties Interactors and Interactables together within a scene, so any Interactor can potentially act on any compatible Interactable without them needing to know about each other directly.

Two Interactor types recur across the toolkit's documentation and directly reflect the two ways a real hand or controller can engage an object: a Direct Interactor detects and acts on objects it physically touches or overlaps (its 'interaction zone'), while a Ray Interactor casts a ray outward to detect and act on objects at a distance — the same raycasting concept introduced in Module 8, now applied specifically to grabbing rather than just clicking.

What Makes an Object Grabbable

The toolkit's own grab-interactable documentation is specific about the required components: a grabbable object needs a Rigidbody (the fundamental physics object that lets the engine's physics simulation govern it once released), a Collider (defining its shape for the purposes of overlap and hit detection), and the Grab Interactable component itself, which manages the state transitions of being hovered, selected (grabbed), and released. Community walkthroughs of the toolkit describe the practical setup pattern the same way: add a Rigidbody to the target object, then add the Grab Interactable component, so the object can be picked up by either a Direct or Ray Interactor and, once released, fall back under normal physics control.

Code Concepts & Examples

These snippets illustrate the underlying idea in plain JavaScript / React Three Fiber, the same environment used by the platform's in-browser editor. They are simplified teaching examples, not the completion-check solution itself.

Zone-entry detection + grab-and-release state machine

Mirrors the Interactor/Interactable relationship: detect overlap, then manage hover -> grabbed -> released states.

function useHandInteraction(handRef, targetRef, interactionRadius = 0.5) {

  const [isInZone, setIsInZone] = useState(false);

  const [isGrabbed, setIsGrabbed] = useState(false);



  useFrame(() => {

    const distance = handRef.current.position.distanceTo(targetRef.current.position);

    setIsInZone(distance < interactionRadius);

  });



  function onGrabInput() {

    if (isInZone) setIsGrabbed(true);

  }



  function onReleaseInput() {

    if (isGrabbed) setIsGrabbed(false);

  }



  useFrame(() => {

    if (isGrabbed) {

      // While grabbed, object position follows the hand (attached)

      targetRef.current.position.copy(handRef.current.position);

    }

  });



  return { isInZone, isGrabbed, onGrabInput, onReleaseInput };

}



Key Terms

Interactor

A component that defines how a GameObject (e.g. a hand or controller) can interact with other objects in a scene.

Interactable

A component that defines how a given object can be interacted with (grabbed, moved, dropped, etc.).

Direct Interactor

An interactor that detects and acts on objects it physically overlaps at close range.

Ray Interactor

An interactor that casts a ray to detect and act on objects at a distance.

Grab Interactable

A component that manages an object's hover, select (grab), and release states, typically requiring a Rigidbody and Collider.



Common Pitfalls

Snapping the grabbed object exactly to the hand's origin instead of preserving the offset at the moment of grab — this makes objects visibly 'jump' the instant they're picked up.

Forgetting to hand control back to physics on release — a grabbed object should resume falling/colliding normally once released, not stay frozen in place.

How This Grounds the Module's Visualization & Task

The module's visualization — a simulated hand/controller moved by mouse drag that highlights an object when its interaction zone overlaps it, then visibly attaches the object to the hand on grab until release — is a mouse-driven simplification of exactly the Direct Interactor + Grab Interactable relationship documented in Unity's toolkit, deliberately built as a conceptual simulation rather than requiring real XR hardware, consistent with the platform's browser-only design.

The task (detect when the hand/controller enters the object's interaction zone, then implement a grab-and-release behavior) mirrors the toolkit's real component responsibilities one-for-one: zone-entry detection plays the role of the Interactor's hover/select detection, and the grab-and-release logic plays the role of the Grab Interactable's state machine, extending Module 8's simple click-to-toggle into a full multi-stage interaction sequence.

