Grounded in: Unity Learn — Physics fundamentals (Rigidbody, collision detection)



Learning Objectives

Distinguish a static collider from a dynamic rigidbody.

Explain how gravity, mass, and restitution affect physics-driven motion.

Understand why this module is the only one in the set requiring a new dependency (a physics engine).

Static Colliders vs. Dynamic Rigidbodies

Unity's physics documentation draws a clean line between two categories of physical object. A static collider is a Collider component with no Rigidbody attached — it can be hit and collided with, but it never moves as a result of physics, and common real-world equivalents are walls, floors, and ramps. A dynamic collider, by contrast, is a Collider paired with a Rigidbody, which is the fundamental component that subjects a GameObject to the physics engine — once attached, gravity and other forces can act on the object, and it will move, fall, and bounce according to the simulation rather than staying put. Dynamic objects can collide with and be blocked by static objects, but static objects themselves are never pushed by that interaction.

A third middle category, kinematic rigidbodies, behaves like a static object by default (immune to incoming forces) but can still be moved directly via script or animation — useful for objects that need to interact with the physics world on the developer's terms rather than the simulation's.

Gravity, Mass, and Restitution

Once an object has a Rigidbody, several exposed properties govern how it behaves under simulation: mass determines its resistance to being moved by a given force, drag slows its linear movement over time (roughly simulating air resistance), and a 'use gravity' toggle determines whether the global gravity setting affects it at all. Bounciness, exposed through a Physic Material assigned to a collider, determines how much kinetic energy is retained (versus absorbed) in a collision — a high-restitution material will make an object rebound with most of its speed intact, while a low-restitution one will make it land with a dull thud and barely bounce.

Why This Module Needs a New Dependency

Everything covered from Module 1 through Module 9 runs on the platform's existing browser pipeline (React Three Fiber, Monaco editor, controlled JavaScript execution). Rigid-body physics simulation, however, is computationally involved enough that it is standard practice to delegate it to a dedicated physics engine rather than hand-rolling it, which is why this module is the only one in the set that requires adding a new dependency to the stack — a React Three Fiber-compatible physics engine such as @react-three/rapier or @react-three/cannon — to provide real rigidbody and collision simulation in the browser.

Code Concepts & Examples

These snippets illustrate the underlying idea in plain JavaScript / React Three Fiber, the same environment used by the platform's in-browser editor. They are simplified teaching examples, not the completion-check solution itself.

Static floor vs. dynamic ball, with gravity and restitution

Uses @react-three/cannon; @react-three/rapier follows the same static/dynamic split with a slightly different API.

import { usePlane, useSphere } from '@react-three/cannon';



function Floor() {

  // No mass specified -> static collider, never moves under physics.

  const [ref] = usePlane(() => ({

    rotation: [-Math.PI / 2, 0, 0],

    position: [0, 0, 0],

  }));

  return <mesh ref={ref}><planeGeometry args={[10, 10]} /></mesh>;

}



function Ball({ gravityScale, restitution }) {

  const [ref, api] = useSphere(() => ({

    mass: 1,                 // mass > 0 -> dynamic, affected by gravity/forces

    position: [0, 5, 0],

    args: [0.5],

    material: { restitution }, // bounciness

  }));



  return (

    <mesh ref={ref}>

      <sphereGeometry args={[0.5, 32, 32]} />

      <meshStandardMaterial color="crimson" />

    </mesh>

  );

}



Task: apply a force so the ball reaches a target zone after bouncing off a wall

function launchBallToTarget(api, targetX) {

  // A single impulse; the wall collision (handled by the physics engine)

  // will redirect the ball's velocity on contact, and restitution

  // determines how much speed survives the bounce.

  api.applyImpulse([targetX > 0 ? 2 : -2, 3, 0], [0, 0, 0]);

}



function checkInTargetZone(ballPosition, zoneCenter, zoneRadius) {

  const dx = ballPosition.x - zoneCenter.x;

  const dz = ballPosition.z - zoneCenter.z;

  return Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dz * dz) <= zoneRadius;

}



Key Terms

Static collider

A Collider component without a Rigidbody; can be collided with but never moves due to physics.

Rigidbody

The core Unity physics component that subjects a GameObject to forces such as gravity and collision response.

Kinematic rigidbody

A Rigidbody that ignores incoming physics forces by default but can still be moved directly via script or animation.

Restitution / bounciness

How much kinetic energy an object retains after a collision, governing how much it rebounds.

Collision detection mode

A Rigidbody setting (Discrete, Continuous, Continuous Dynamic) controlling how carefully fast-moving objects are checked for passing through other colliders.



Common Pitfalls

Giving the floor a mass (making it dynamic) by accident — a floor that responds to gravity/collisions like a ball will sink or fly away instead of staying put.

Setting restitution above 1 or expecting bounces to never lose energy — real (and simulated) collisions dissipate some energy; restitution close to 1 approximates a superball, not a return to the exact same height forever.

Reading the object's position for a 'target reached' check on the same frame the impulse was applied — physics needs to actually settle over several frames before a resting-position check is meaningful.

